{
  "requirement": "{cite}`requirements.sdk.domain`\n\nThe {term}`requirements.sdk.application_domain` defines its central `Error`\nassociated type on `Domain`. Node reads, edge queries, and evidence persistence\nuse it to report internal errors:\n\n```rust,ignore\n// Defined in trust_but_verify::domain.\npub trait Domain: Clone + Send + Sync {\n    // ...\n\n    /// Application-defined errors from domain operations.\n    type Error: Send;\n\n    // ...\n}\n```",
  "determination": true,
  "rationale": "`requirements.sdk.errors` says `Domain::Error` is declared as `type Error: Send;`. That means the bound is exactly `Send`, and the error is used in node reads, edge queries and evidence persistence. `requirements.sdk.domain` is cited as context only.\n\nThe only reviewed test is `crates/trust-but-verify/tests/contracts.rs#fn[domain_relationships_accept_opaque_types_and_send_only_errors]`. It carries a `{test}` reference to `requirements.sdk.errors`.\n\n- **Error is `Send` (loosening the bound):** The nested helper `check_bounds` is generic over `G: Domain` and calls `error::<G::Error>()`. `error` is declared `fn error<T: Send>()`. If `Send` were dropped from the declared bound, this generic code would no longer compile.\n- **Error is not required to be more than `Send` (tightening the bound):** The fixture `Relationships` uses `type Error = GraphError`, and `GraphError` is `struct GraphError(Cell<u8>)`. It is `Send` but not `Sync`. A `Sync` bound added to `Domain::Error` would make the fixture's `impl Domain` fail to compile. The same applies to any `Clone` or `Debug` bound if `GraphError` lacks them. I did not check which traits `GraphError` implements, but `Sync` is the case that matters.\n- **Errors used in node reads, edge queries and evidence persistence:**\n  - `node_content` is called and its result unwrapped.\n  - `outgoing_edges` is exercised on success paths and on an error path. For `Identity(255)` the test unwraps the error and asserts `send(error).0.get() == 255`. That checks the error is produced through `outgoing_edges` and is `Send`.\n  - `load_evidence` and `store_evidence` are driven through `send(...)` and awaited. `check_bounds` also calls `send(graph.load_evidence(...))` and `send(graph.store_evidence(...))` generically. The inline comment notes the futures stay `Send` even though the error is not `Sync`.\n\nThe assertions are compile-time bound checks plus runtime checks of the error value. They would reject the plausible violations: a missing `Send` bound, or an extra `Sync` bound that makes the error type unusable.\n\nNo obligation is untested, and no marking gap exists. The two other `type Error` fixtures I found are not needed for this requirement.",
